==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|luminem|lang=enm}}, {{term|limnen||to illuminate (a manuscript)|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|enluminer|lang=frm}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|illūminō||I illuminate|lang=la}} (English {{term|illuminate|lang=en}}), {{term|in|lang=la}} + {{term|lūminō||light up|lang=la}}, from {{term|lumen|lūmen|light|lang=en}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*lewk-}} (English {{term|leam||glow|lang=en}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|lĭm}}, {{IPA|/lɪm/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪm|lang=en}}
* {{homophones|lang=en|lim|limb}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[draw]] or [[paint]]; [[delineate]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[describe]].
# {{context|transitive|obsolete|lang=en}} To [[illuminate]], as a manuscript.

====Usage notes====
* Particularly used in arts journalism; not understood by significant portion of American public.<ref>[http://www.baltimoresun.com/news/maryland/bs-md-limn-headline-20100907,0,3496485.story The Sun goes out on a limn with unusual headline: Unusual word in front-page headline leaves some readers scratching their heads], Jill Rosen, ''The Baltimore Sun,'' September 7, 2010</ref>

